- id: N:target/rust
  type: target
  name: rust
  summary: sloppy-xml-py weighed for a transconception into Rust; feasible with the standard library alone (the
    patterns written as matchers, the HTML5 table carried as data).
  stance: observed
  language: rust
  version: 1.97.1
  edition: '2021'
  program: sloppy-cases
  library: sloppy-xml
  weighed:
  - point: 1
    score: 3
    evidence: the Rust Foundation since 2021 and the project's teams and RFC process; stable releases every six
      weeks since 1.0 in 2015
  - point: 2
    score: 2
    evidence: the Rust Reference and the Ferrocene Language Specification, neither complete; one production compiler,
      others partial
  - point: 3
    score: 3
    evidence: the 1.0 stability promise and editions
  - point: 4
    score: 3
    evidence: 'every kept reliance provided: 8 built-in, 10 written, no crate, none cannot'
  - point: 5
    score: 3
    evidence: one program and one library on macOS arm64, Linux, Windows; links with the system C linker
  - point: 6
    score: 3
    evidence: a scan over code points, a stack, an ordered list of pairs, events as an enum; the lone surrogate
      ruled changed
  - point: 7
    score: 3
    evidence: memory-safe without unsafe; the spike uses none
  - point: 8
    score: 2
    evidence: exhaustive matching on event kinds and strategies; field types; the judge checks positions and texts
  - point: 9
    score: 2
    evidence: not measured at the weighing (no run); provisional; measure at O8 and O9
  - point: 10
    score: 2
    evidence: no budget stated; 30 seconds a step allowed; linear scan; to be timed at O8
  - point: 11
    score: 2
    evidence: about 1,400 lines in the spike, 2,000 in all
  - point: 12
    score: 3
    evidence: standard library only; the fabulac stays the source
  provides:
  - for: C:reliance/S-R01
    by: written
    what: seventeen hand-written matchers with the patterns' outcomes
    note: the riskiest use; spike told/rust-spike
  - for: C:reliance/S-R02
    by: written
    what: 'the white-space set written out: Unicode white space plus U+001C to U+001F'
  - for: C:reliance/S-R03
    by: built-in
    what: str::trim_matches with that set
  - for: C:reliance/S-R04
    by: built-in
    what: the text as a vector of char; the context window written
    note: where each error's position is taken is not stated in the fabulac
  - for: C:reliance/S-R06
    by: built-in
    what: char::is_ascii_alphanumeric
  - for: C:reliance/S-R07
    by: written
    what: the pinned version's HTML5 table carried as data, looked up as written then with ;
  - for: C:reliance/S-R09
    by: written
    what: a digit-count check then u64::from_str_radix; past U+10FFFF kept as written
    note: 'the lost # ruled changed'
  - for: C:reliance/S-R10
    by: built-in
    what: char::from_u32, U+FFFD for a surrogate
    note: ruled changed
  - for: C:reliance/S-R11
    by: written
    what: the control characters and the eight typographic fixes in order
  - for: C:reliance/S-R12
    by: written
    what: a vector of pairs replaced in place
  - for: C:reliance/S-R13
    by: built-in
    what: str::find and String::replace_range
  - for: C:reliance/S-R15
    by: written
    what: an element type with tag, attributes, text, tail, children
    note: alike in Rust's own types
  - for: C:reliance/S-R17
    by: written
    what: latin-1 when UTF-8 fails
    note: ruled changed
  - for: C:reliance/S-R18
    by: written
    what: bytes where text is expected refused
    note: ruled changed
  - for: C:reliance/S-R19
    by: built-in
    what: an enum; CUSTOM as STRICT, AGGRESSIVE as LENIENT
  - for: C:reliance/S-R20
    by: written
    what: a second, lxml-like builder in Rust's own types
    note: alike; an unknown name refused, exit only
  - for: C:reliance/S-R21
    by: built-in
    what: an Iterator, or the whole list
    note: alike, exit only
  - for: C:reliance/S-R22
    by: built-in
    what: an enum with named fields
    note: alike
  - for: C:reliance/whole-numbers-unbounded
    by: library
    what: num-bigint, or i128 where the program's numbers stay small
    note: the standard library has no unbounded whole number; i64 overflow panics in a debug build and wraps in
      a release build
    gap: num-bigint pinned in Cargo.lock and fetched once, or the numbers held in i128 with a refusal past it, ruled
      by the transconceiver
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/number-from-text
    by: built-in
    what: str::parse::<i64> after trimming
    note: underscores between digits, signs and non-ASCII digits the original accepts are glue to write
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/number-as-text
    by: written
    what: the printed form of a number around f64's Display
    note: 'Display gives the shortest digits but never an exponent, Debug writes 1e16: where the form switches to
      an exponent, and inf and nan, are written'
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/conversions
    by: written
    what: each conversion the program makes, with the original's refusals
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/value-kinds
    by: written
    what: the kind names the original prints, written as words
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-length-in-code-points
    by: built-in
    what: str::chars().count()
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-indexed-by-code-point
    by: built-in
    what: a Vec<char>
    note: a str indexes bytes; a position the program shows is counted in chars
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-order-by-code-point
    by: built-in
    what: str's Ord
    note: UTF-8 bytes order as their code points do
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/what-is-a-space
    by: written
    what: char::is_whitespace with U+001C to U+001F added
    note: the set is written out
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-split-on-space
    by: built-in
    what: str::split with the written space test
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-replace
    by: built-in
    what: str::replace, str::replacen
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/code-points-by-number
    by: built-in
    what: char::from_u32
    note: it refuses surrogates
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/printed-form
    by: written
    what: the original's printed form of texts, lists and maps
    note: Debug prints differently
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/truth-of-values
    by: written
    what: each test of truth written out
    note: 'Rust has no truth of values: a test names what it tests'
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/any-all-stop-early
    by: built-in
    what: Iterator::any, Iterator::all
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/map-keeps-order
    by: written
    what: a Vec of pairs, replaced in place
    note: HashMap keeps no order; indexmap would do it as a library
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/set-members
    by: built-in
    what: HashSet, BTreeSet
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/set-order-varies
    by: built-in
    what: HashSet iteration
    note: its order varies from run to run as well; only a sort makes it repeatable
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/membership-identity-then-equality
    by: written
    what: == on values, with the identity cases written
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/records-compare-by-fields
    by: built-in
    what: derive(PartialEq)
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/generators-lazy
    by: built-in
    what: an Iterator
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/error-text
    by: built-in
    what: Display of the error
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/error-kinds
    by: written
    what: an enum of the program's errors, matched by kind
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/optional-library
    by: written
    what: a cargo feature with the part that needs the library behind it
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/missing-key-error
    by: written
    what: the message naming the key
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/text-decoding-strict
    by: built-in
    what: String::from_utf8
    note: the error the original raises is written
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/bytes-decoded
    by: built-in
    what: Vec<u8> and String
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/pattern-syntax
    by: written
    what: each pattern written as a small matcher with the same outcome
    note: the standard library has no regular expressions; the regex crate has no look-around
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/pattern-unicode-classes
    by: written
    what: the Unicode classes in each matcher (char::is_alphanumeric and the written space test)
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/pattern-flags
    by: written
    what: case folding, line anchors and dot-all in each matcher
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/html5-entities
    by: written
    what: the table carried as data and looked up as the original does
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/tree-text-and-tail
    by: written
    what: an element type with its text and tail
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/runtime-version
    by: built-in
    what: the edition and rust-version of Cargo.toml
    answered: rules
  - for: C:reliance/pytest-conventions
    by: dropped
    what: 'nothing: cargo test reads its own names'
    note: the original's test runner's names mean nothing to the new program
    answered: rules
  spike:
    use: the patterns (C:reliance/S-R01) on P:case/x0.S-C05 and P:case/x0.S-C06
    level: 3
    where: _x0/sloppy-xml-py/told/rust-spike/
    judged-by: O8 (x0.sh build and judge, label rust-spike)
    evidence:
      level: 3
      against: original
      cases:
      - P:case/x0.S-C05
      - P:case/x0.S-C06
      differ:
        ruled: 2
        unruled: 0
      where:
        file: ../results/2026-11-18-1819/sloppy-xml-py/rust-spike/verdict.json
        digest: 7aba17502f2d84f86e10016adb636cc99762930f540b0ae810b04a344f987817
      at: 2026-11-18T18:19
  verdict: feasible
  conditions:
  - the spike agrees on S-C05 and on S-C06 step 0 at O8 (spike level 2 or better on its cases)
  at: '2026-11-18'
  by: R:maker/agent-w1-xp
  made:
    move: weigh
    in: rust
    by-hand: true
    step: A7
    run: x0-A7-2026-11-18
    engine: model:claude-opus-5-5
  provenance:
    mode: manual
    confidence: 1
    sources:
    - _x0/sloppy-xml-py/sloppy-xml-py.fabulac.cosm
    - _x0/sitting-1.md
    - _x0/sloppy-xml-py/rulings.json
    - orangepaper "Transcoding through the fabulac", tab The paper, "Choosing the target"
    - rows for the reliances 0.8's ground raised, by `weigh`'s rules from the target table (promote_x0.py --ground,
      2026-11-19); every row A7 wrote is as it wrote it
